    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Luddites were not opposed to machines as such, and many were skilled machine operators; they attacked the machines of manufacturers who were circumventing the standard labour practices of the time.</li><li>Each region wrecked the machines that threatened its own trade: wide knitting frames in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, steam-powered looms in the North West, and shearing frames and gig mills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>.</li><li>The government answered with 12,000 troops, made machine-breaking a capital crime in 1812 and staged a mass trial at York in 1813, whose executions and transportations quickly ended the movement.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> croppers broke the cropping machines made by Enoch Taylor of Marsden with a hammer they called “Enoch”, purportedly shouting, “Enoch made them, and Enoch shall break them.”</p></aside>
    </section>

