    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1811</span><span class="rf-text">The Luddite movement begins at Arnold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, and spreads rapidly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> condemns the repression of the textile workers in a speech in the House of Lords</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text">The Destruction of Stocking Frames, etc. Act makes machine-breaking a capital crime</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1813</span><span class="rf-text">A mass trial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> charges over 60 men; the sentences of execution and transportation quickly end the movement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1816</span><span class="rf-text">Activists smash Heathcote’s lace-making machine in Loughborough</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">Jeremiah Brandreth, a probable ex-Luddite, leads the Pentrich Rising</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text">The Swing Riots in southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, an agricultural variant of Luddism, break threshing machines</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1996</span><span class="rf-text">A Second Luddite Congress at Barnesville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, issues a manifesto of neo-Luddism</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoleonic_Wars_(revision).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 18th century the “domestic” manufacturers were notorious for precarious work. Underemployment was chronic, and it was common to keep a larger workforce than needed as insurance against labour shortages in boom times. The organisation of the textile industry by merchant capitalists was inherently unstable: with their capital still largely in raw materials, they could easily take on work when trade was good and cut back when it was bad, and they lacked the later factory owners’ interest in a steady rate of production. Seasonal swings in wages and violent short-term fluctuations caused by harvests and war produced periodic outbreaks of violence.</p><p>Machine-breaking had a long history, especially in the hosiery and woollen trades. Stockingers had taken organised action at various times since 1675, and new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>-spinning technologies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, which could be worked by less-skilled, low-wage labourers, met violent resistance in 1768 and 1779. Parliament answered with acts such as the Protection of Stocking Frames, etc. Act 1788. Rising food prices caused riots too, among the Keelmen on the Tyne in 1710 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> miners at Falmouth in 1727.</p><p>The Luddite movement itself emerged in the harsh economic climate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. The British economy suffered greatly from 1810 to 1812, with high unemployment and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, caused by the cost of the wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a> of economic warfare and an escalating conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Luddites protested against certain types of automated machinery because of their concerns over workers’ pay, labour savings, child labour, working conditions and the quality of output. They were not opposed to machines as such, and many were skilled operators in the textile industry; they attacked manufacturers who were trying to circumvent the standard labour practices of the time. Each region wrecked the machinery that threatened its own trade: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> the “wide” knitting frames used to make cheap and inferior lace articles, in the North West the steam-powered looms that threatened wages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> trade, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> the shearing frames and gig mills used to finish woollen cloth.</p><p>Many Luddite groups were highly organised and used machine-breaking as one of several tools for specific political ends. Their letters to industrialists and officials explained why they destroyed machinery and threatened more if the “obnoxious” machines stayed in use. The Midlands Luddites appealed to the legitimacy of the Company of Framework Knitters; in the North West, which lacked such institutions, letters sought recognition as a united body of tradespeople, asked for higher minimum wages and an end to child labour, and used radical language linking the movement to the American and French revolutionaries.</p><p>The historian Malcolm I. Thomis argued that the attacks implied no hostility to machinery as such, and Eric Hobsbawm called them “collective bargaining by riot”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement began at Arnold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, on 11 March 1811 and spread rapidly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> over the next two years. Its main areas of operation were Nottinghamshire from November 1811, the West Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> from early 1812, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>. Luddites met at night on the moors around the industrial towns to practise military-like drills and manoeuvres, and besides their raids they organised public demonstrations and sent letters to industrialists and officials. In Yorkshire the letters turned to more violent threats against local authorities.</p><p>Luddites clashed with government troops at Burton’s Mill in Middleton and at Westhoughton Mill, both in Lancashire, and mill and factory owners took to shooting protesters. Four Luddites led by George Mellor ambushed and killed the mill owner William Horsfall of Ottiwells Mill in Marsden at Crosland Moor in Huddersfield; Horsfall had said that he would “ride up to his saddle in Luddite blood”. The Luddites and their supporters sent anonymous death threats to magistrates and food merchants, and may have attacked some.</p><p>After an attack on Cartwright’s Mill at Rawfolds near Cleckheaton, the government staged a mass trial at York in January 1813. Activists still smashed Heathcote’s lace-making machine in Loughborough in 1816, and he and other industrialists built secret chambers in their buildings to hide in during an attack. In 1817 Jeremiah Brandreth led the Pentrich Rising, a general uprising unrelated to machinery that can be seen as the last major Luddite act.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-byron.jpg" alt="Lord Byron" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byron_1813_by_Phillips.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Phillips, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Luddites called themselves followers of “Ned Ludd”, a legendary weaver whose name was used as a pseudonym in threatening letters to mill owners and government officials; the name occurs in their writings as early as 1811. Ned Ludd was said to be an apprentice who smashed two stocking frames in 1779 after being criticised and told to change his method. He often appears as Captain, General or King Ludd, and the legends place his home either in Anstey, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, or in Sherwood Forest.</p><p>Most Luddites were textile workers, many of them skilled. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> the croppers, who faced mass unemployment when Enoch Taylor of Marsden introduced cropping machines, broke the machines with a hammer they called “Enoch” to mock him. George Mellor fired the shot that killed William Horsfall; of the four men arrested, Benjamin Walker turned informant and the other three were hanged. Jeremiah Brandreth, who led the Pentrich Rising of 1817, was an unemployed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> stockinger and probably a former Luddite.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> was one of the few prominent defenders of the Luddites. In the House of Lords on 27 February 1812 he denounced the plight of the working class and the government’s ruthless repression, saying he had never, even in the most oppressed provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, seen “such squalid wretchedness” as in the heart of a Christian country, and he opposed the law that made machine-breaking a capital crime.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington.jpg" alt="Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Arthur_Wellesley,_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Lawrence, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Luddites won none of their demands. The crisis of 1810 to 1812 led to widespread protest and violence, but the middle and upper classes strongly supported the government, which used the army to suppress all working-class unrest, and the Luddites above all. Some 12,000 government troops, most of them militia or yeomanry, were involved, a larger number, Eric Hobsbawm wrote, than the army the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a> led into Portugal in 1808 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>.</p><p>Parliament made machine-breaking a capital crime with the Destruction of Stocking Frames, etc. Act 1812. At the mass trial at York in January 1813 the government charged over 60 men, among them Mellor and his companions, with crimes connected with Luddism. Some were Luddites, but many had no connection to the movement. The proceedings were legitimate jury trials, yet many cases were abandoned for lack of evidence and 30 men were acquitted. The trials were meant as show trials to deter others, and the harsh sentences of those found guilty, which included execution and penal transportation, quickly ended the movement.</p><p>Historians have explained the tactic in different ways. Thomis saw machine-breaking as one of the very few tactics that workers had to put pressure on employers, undermine lower-paid rivals and build solidarity. Hobsbawm explained it as a tactic used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> since the Restoration, because manufactories were scattered across the country and large-scale strikes were impractical.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Machine-breaking did not end with the Luddites. An agricultural variant of Luddism occurred in the widespread Swing Riots of 1830 in southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which centred on breaking threshing machines. The Luddites are remembered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> folk song “The Cropper Lads”, recorded by Lou Killen and Maddy Prior among others, and in the traditional song “The Triumph of General Ludd”, which Chumbawamba recorded for their 1988 album English Rebel Songs.</p><p>Their name took on a life of its own. Today “Luddite” is sometimes used for anyone who opposes or resists new technologies. In a British parliamentary debate in 1956 a Labour spokesman said that organised workers were by no means wedded to a “Luddite Philosophy”. By 2006 the term neo-Luddism had emerged for opposition to many forms of technology; a manifesto of the Second Luddite Congress, held at Barnesville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in April 1996, called it “a leaderless movement of passive resistance to consumerism and the increasingly bizarre and frightening technologies of the Computer Age”.</p><p>Economists speak of the “Luddite fallacy”, the fear that technological unemployment must cause lasting structural unemployment. Through the 20th century and the first decade of the 21st the dominant view among economists was that it was indeed a fallacy, but more recently there has been growing support for the view that the benefits of automation are not equally shared.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Luddites were English textile workers who broke machines in organised raids between 1811 and 1816, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and the North West, over pay, conditions and the quality of work. Troops, a law making machine-breaking a capital crime and the York trials of 1813 suppressed them, and their name came to mean any opponent of new technology.</em></p>

